          <header>
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Bifrost 2026: The Open-Source AI Gateway That Goes 50&times; Faster Than the Status Quo</h1>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
              <time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time>
              <span>•</span>
              <span>Maxim Provider Review</span>
              <span>•</span>
              <span>About 12 min read</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Most "open-source AI gateway" projects in 2026 are still Python dispatchers — useful, but slow at the kind of sustained traffic that real production agents generate. <strong>Bifrost</strong> (by Maxim, the AI evaluation / observability company) is the first one to take the gateway itself seriously as a performance problem. The repo <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">maximhq/bifrost</code> is Apache-2.0, written in Go, ships as a single binary / Docker image / npx package, and unifies <strong>23+ upstream providers and 1,000+ models</strong> behind one OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Maxim's published benchmarks put it at <strong>50&times; the throughput of LiteLLM at 500 RPS</strong>, with <strong>68% lower memory</strong>, and under <strong>100 microseconds of added latency at 5,000 RPS</strong>. This review covers the verified pricing (OSS is free forever; Enterprise is custom), the deployment surface (Docker, K8s, Go binary, npx), and the regulatory positioning — VPC / on-prem / air-gapped — that is making Bifrost the default gateway for teams that need OpenRouter's coverage but cannot put their prompts on a hosted SaaS.
            </p>
          </header>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-400 p-4 rounded-r">
            <p class="text-blue-800 text-sm">
              <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Bifrost (maximhq/bifrost) is an Apache-2.0 AI gateway in Go, published by Maxim (H3 Labs Inc., San Francisco). Verified live on 2026-09-08: <strong>7,869 GitHub stars</strong>, 1,176 forks, last commit the same day, license is genuine Apache-2.0 (LICENSE file confirms). The OSS tier is <strong>free forever with no markup on upstream model prices</strong>; you self-host it on Docker, Kubernetes, a Go binary, or <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">npx -y @maximhq/bifrost</code>. Out of the box it routes across OpenAI, Anthropic, AWS Bedrock, Google Vertex, Azure OpenAI, Cohere, Mistral, Groq, Cerebras, Ollama, Hugging Face, Replicate, and custom vLLM/SGLang/Ollama endpoints — <strong>23+ providers, 1,000+ models</strong>, all reachable through a single OpenAI-compatible <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">/v1/chat/completions</code> endpoint. Published benchmarks: 50&times; LiteLLM at 500 RPS, 68% less memory, &lt;100µs added latency at 5,000 RPS. Built-in automatic failover, weighted load balancing, semantic caching, virtual-key budget control, MCP gateway, and OTel-compatible metrics/traces. Enterprise adds SAML/OIDC SSO, Vault integration, adaptive load balancing, cluster mode, guardrails, log exports, and audit logs; pricing is custom via a 14-day free trial. <strong>There is no public hosted SaaS endpoint</strong> — every customer runs Bifrost in their own VPC, on-prem, or air-gapped. Mainland China teams self-host on Alibaba/Huawei/Tencent Cloud with a domestic Ollama or vLLM upstream to keep every prompt byte on-shore.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

            <h2>What Is Bifrost?</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost is a high-performance <strong>AI gateway</strong> built by Maxim, the San Francisco-based AI evaluation company (H3 Labs Inc.) behind the Maxim SDK and Bifrost observability tooling. Where OpenRouter is a hosted SaaS that aggregates model providers behind one bill, and where LiteLLM is a Python dispatcher library, Bifrost sits in between: it is <strong>self-hosted open source</strong> with a Go-based data plane, runs in your own infrastructure, and behaves like a production-grade gateway product rather than a developer convenience library. The repo <a href="https://github.com/maximhq/bifrost" target="_blank" rel="noopener">maximhq/bifrost</a> is published under Apache-2.0, has 7,869 stars and 1,176 forks as of September 8, 2026, and the most recent commit landed the same day, which means you are not inheriting abandoned software if you adopt it.
            </p>
            <p>
              The gateway exposes a single <strong>OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoint</strong>. That endpoint sits in front of 23+ upstream providers — the official list covers OpenAI, Anthropic, AWS Bedrock, Google Vertex, Azure OpenAI, Cohere, Mistral, Groq, Cerebras, Ollama, Hugging Face, and Replicate, plus any custom self-deployed model you run with vLLM, SGLang, or Ollama. Because Bifrost normalises both model naming (using prefixes like <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">openai/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">anthropic/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">vertex/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">bedrock/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">ollama/</code>) and the request/response format, the same client code routes to any of the <strong>1,000+ models</strong> the gateway exposes — you do not have to maintain provider-specific SDKs in your application.
            </p>

            <h2>Verified Pricing: OSS Free Forever, Enterprise Custom</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost's pricing model is straightforward and worth pinning down because it is fundamentally different from every hosted aggregator on APIRank.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>OSS (open source) tier — $0.</strong> The OSS tier is published under Apache-2.0 and is free forever. Upstream model token prices pass through with <strong>no markup</strong> — you pay OpenAI list price, Anthropic list price, Google Vertex list price, whatever the upstream charges, and Bifrost adds nothing on top. Your total cost is therefore the upstream model's bill plus whatever you pay to run the gateway itself (a single 2-core VPS is enough for most teams, more for very high QPS). There is no per-token platform fee, no monthly subscription, and no usage cap.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Enterprise — custom via sales demo.</strong> Enterprise unlocks SAML and OIDC SSO, HashiCorp Vault integration, MCP with federated auth, adaptive load balancing, cluster mode, guardrails, log exports, and audit logs. Pricing is custom (the official pricing page reads "Custom pricing, book a demo"), and there is a <strong>14-day free trial</strong>. Enterprise deployments support VPC, on-prem, and air-gapped environments, which is the single biggest reason enterprise teams choose Bifrost over OpenRouter or Helicone Cloud.
            </p>
            <p>
              The honest caveat: the OSS tier has <strong>no unified billing / reconciliation dashboard</strong> — you see your token spend through each provider's own console, not through Bifrost's UI. Enterprise adds the dashboard, but if you are an OSS-only deployment and want spend-by-team reporting you will need to wire your own observability on top of the OTel traces Bifrost already emits.
            </p>

            <h2>Why 50&times; Faster Than LiteLLM Matters</h2>
            <p>
              LiteLLM is the de facto open-source AI gateway today, and it is genuinely useful — but its data plane is Python, which means a per-request GIL hop, an interpreter dispatch overhead, and a memory profile that does not scale linearly. For a small project that is invisible. For a team running thousands of agent requests per second, it shows up as a tail-latency cliff.
            </p>
            <p>
              Bifrost is written in <strong>Go</strong> and treats the gateway as a hot-path performance problem rather than a developer convenience. Maxim's published benchmarks report <strong>50&times; the throughput of LiteLLM at 500 RPS sustained</strong>, <strong>68% lower memory footprint</strong>, and <strong>under 100 microseconds of added latency at 5,000 RPS</strong>. Those numbers come from a sustained 5,000 RPS test on a representative mix of upstream providers, and the gateway does not break a sweat at that rate. In other words, Bifrost's overhead is so low that the latency budget of any request is dominated by the upstream model API you select, not by the gateway itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              The practical impact is that Bifrost is the first open-source gateway that <strong>production agent fleets</strong> — autonomous coders, RAG pipelines, multi-step tool-use agents — can route through without paying a meaningful latency tax. That is not a small thing; for any team that has tried to put LiteLLM in front of a high-RPS agent workload, the gateway becomes the bottleneck.
            </p>

            <h2>Deployment Surface: Docker, K8s, Go Binary, npx</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost ships four distribution channels, and you can be running a fully functional local gateway in under a minute. The fastest path is <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">npx -y @maximhq/bifrost</code> from any directory with Node.js, which downloads and starts the gateway on port 8080. From there you open <a href="http://localhost:8080" target="_blank" rel="noopener">http://localhost:8080</a> in a browser to configure providers, set up virtual keys, and watch real-time traffic. For containerised production deployments there is <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">docker run -p 8080:8080 maximhq/bifrost</code>, a Helm chart for Kubernetes, and a standalone Go binary you can drop on any Linux host.
            </p>
            <p>
              The first API call is OpenAI-compatible and works with the curl <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">/v1/chat/completions</code> route:
            </p>
            <pre class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code is:raw>curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "openai/gpt-4o-mini",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Hello, Bifrost!"}]
  }'</code></pre>
            <p>
              Swap <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">openai/</code> for <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">anthropic/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">vertex/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">bedrock/</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">ollama/</code>, or any other registered prefix and the same call routes to a different upstream provider without changing the client. That is the OpenAI-compatibility value proposition in its sharpest form.
            </p>

            <h2>Failover, Load Balancing, Caching, MCP</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost is not just a router — it is a gateway with the operations features that a hosted aggregator provides, implemented in OSS. The headline features:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Automatic failover.</strong> Per-route failover across providers — if OpenAI is degraded for you, Anthropic takes over without client retry glue. Weights are configurable per route so you can build cost-optimised or quality-optimised routing policies.</li>
              <li><strong>Weighted load balancing.</strong> Distribute traffic across providers according to weights — useful for keeping under a per-provider rate limit or for staying within enterprise contract caps.</li>
              <li><strong>Semantic caching.</strong> Built-in semantic cache reduces token spend on repeated or near-duplicate prompts. This is a real production feature, not a marketing slide — the same client call returns instantly from cache when the prompt is semantically equivalent to a recent one.</li>
              <li><strong>Virtual keys.</strong> Per-team / per-customer budget isolation and rate limits through virtual keys. If you are building a multi-tenant SaaS that exposes LLM calls, this is the feature that lets you bill customers accurately without giving them your root provider key.</li>
              <li><strong>MCP gateway.</strong> Bifrost is itself an MCP gateway — the repo carries <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">mcp-gateway</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">mcp-client</code>, and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">mcp-server</code> tags on GitHub — so MCP tool calls route through the same governance layer as ordinary LLM calls.</li>
              <li><strong>OTel-compatible observability.</strong> Metrics and traces emit in OpenTelemetry format, which plugs straight into Datadog, Honeycomb, Grafana, or Prometheus without glue code.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>China Access and Data Residency</h2>
            <p>
              Because Bifrost is self-hosted software, there is no mainland-China-specific hosted endpoint to discuss. The pattern that works in production is straightforward: deploy Bifrost on a domestic cloud (Alibaba Cloud, Huawei Cloud, or Tencent Cloud), configure it to route only to a self-hosted upstream such as Ollama or vLLM running Qwen, GLM, or DeepSeek weights inside China, and you get the same unified OpenAI-compatible interface that global developers get, while every prompt byte stays on-shore. This is the cleanest setup for teams that need to satisfy data-residency, MLPS, or contract-level obligations without abandoning the modern LLM tooling stack.
            </p>
            <p>
              Bifrost's own overhead is sub-100 microseconds at 5,000 RPS regardless of deployment region, so the latency budget of any request is dominated by the upstream model API you select, not by the gateway itself. A typical mainland setup — Bifrost on Alibaba Cloud in cn-shanghai routing to a vLLM-served Qwen3 endpoint in the same region — sits comfortably under 50 ms total for the gateway hop, and the rest of the latency is the upstream inference itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              Two caveats for mainland teams: there is no official Maxim-hosted demo or mirror inside China, so you cannot use the official quickstart to evaluate Bifrost without a VPN. And the OSS tier does not include SAML SSO or Vault integration — for any enterprise with audit and credential-rotation requirements you will need the Enterprise tier.
            </p>

            <h2>Bifrost vs LiteLLM vs OpenRouter vs Cloudflare AI Gateway</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost's closest peers are <strong>LiteLLM</strong> (the open-source Python dispatcher), <strong>OpenRouter</strong> (the hosted multi-provider router), <strong>Cloudflare AI Gateway</strong> (CF Workers-based gateway), and <strong>Portkey</strong> (the hosted observability-focused gateway). Where Bifrost fits:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>vs LiteLLM</strong> — same OSS spirit, same self-host deployment, but Bifrost is in Go and benchmarks at 50&times; the throughput with 68% less memory. Pick Bifrost if you have any production RPS above a few hundred. Pick LiteLLM if you want the most mature provider coverage in a single library.</li>
              <li><strong>vs OpenRouter</strong> — OpenRouter is hosted, you pay OpenRouter's markup (which varies by model, generally 5% or so) and your prompts transit OpenRouter's servers. Bifrost is self-hosted, free, and zero markup, but you operate it yourself. Pick OpenRouter if you want zero ops. Pick Bifrost if you cannot put prompts on a third-party SaaS.</li>
              <li><strong>vs Cloudflare AI Gateway</strong> — CF AI Gateway is the easiest gateway if you are already on Cloudflare Workers; it has zero markup and a generous free tier. The downside is CF-specific lock-in (KV/Durable Objects for caching, CF Logs for observability). Bifrost runs anywhere — on your laptop, on bare metal, in any cloud — and emits standard OTel.</li>
              <li><strong>vs Portkey</strong> — Portkey is the strongest hosted competitor on observability and governance. Bifrost's OSS tier does not include the dashboard Portkey ships by default; Enterprise adds the equivalent. Pick Portkey if you want a hosted product with strong governance. Pick Bifrost if you want to own every byte of the gateway.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Who Should Use Bifrost?</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost is the right fit when you tick at least two of these boxes:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>You need OSS-first self-hosting.</strong> Your prompts cannot transit a third-party SaaS, whether for regulatory, contractual, or cost reasons. Bifrost runs in your VPC, your on-prem cluster, or your air-gapped environment.</li>
              <li><strong>You have high-RPS agent workloads.</strong> Autonomous coders, tool-calling agents, multi-step RAG pipelines. Bifrost's 50&times; LiteLLM throughput matters when you are running tens of thousands of requests per minute.</li>
              <li><strong>You want zero markup on upstream model prices.</strong> Bifrost passes the upstream list price through transparently. No 5% platform fee, no premium tier, no usage cap.</li>
              <li><strong>You need MCP governance.</strong> Bifrost is itself an MCP gateway, so MCP tool calls route through the same authentication, rate limiting, and observability as ordinary LLM calls.</li>
              <li><strong>You are on a regulated stack.</strong> Finance, healthcare, government, defence, or any industry where every prompt byte must stay inside your own infrastructure. Bifrost Enterprise supports VPC / on-prem / air-gapped deployments with SAML SSO and Vault integration.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Bifrost is the wrong fit if you want a zero-ops hosted SaaS (pick OpenRouter or Portkey), if you want the absolute broadest provider catalog without self-hosting (pick OpenRouter), or if your workload is small enough that LiteLLM's Python overhead does not show up.
            </p>

            <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
            <p>
              Bifrost is the most interesting AI infrastructure release of 2026 so far for teams that take self-hosting seriously. The combination of an Apache-2.0 license, a Go-based data plane, 23+ providers and 1,000+ models behind one OpenAI-compatible endpoint, 50&times; LiteLLM throughput at 68% less memory, sub-100µs overhead at 5k RPS, MCP gateway, OTel observability, and an Enterprise tier that supports VPC / on-prem / air-gapped deployments with SSO is hard to match — at any price. The OSS tier is genuinely free and genuinely production-grade; the Enterprise tier is a real enterprise product with sales, contracts, and SLA-backed support.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you are already running LiteLLM and hitting throughput or memory walls, migrate to Bifrost. If you are running OpenRouter and need to keep prompts inside your own infrastructure for regulatory reasons, migrate to Bifrost. If you are running Cloudflare AI Gateway and want to escape CF-specific lock-in for standard OTel, migrate to Bifrost. The migration story is straightforward: Bifrost speaks OpenAI at the client side, so a base-url swap is usually enough.
            </p>

          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Bifrost Maxim FAQ</h2>

            <div class="space-y-6">
